                <pb facs="0240" n="240" sameAs="frus:frus1861/pg240"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Seward to Mr. Dayton</hi>.</head>
                <note rend="inline" type="source">[Extract]</note>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 42.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of
                            State</hi>,<lb/><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Washington</hi>,</placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1861-08-17"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >August</hi> 17, 1861</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: * * * * * * * *</p>
                <p rendition="#flush">You will learn from a distinct despatch, No. 41, which
                    accompanies or which will soon follow this, that our negotiation in England has
                    taken a new phase, which, of course, will soon present itself in discussion with
                    the French government.</p>
                <p>Treason was emboldened by its partial success at Manassas, but the Union now
                    grows manifestly stronger every day. Let us see how Great Britain will
                    explain.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, respectfully, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer><signed>WILLIAM H. SEWARD.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">William L. Dayton</hi>, Esq., <hi
                            rendition="#italic">&amp;c., &amp;c., &amp;c.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
